LEADERSHIP IN WAR by Andrew Roberts

2019. Allen Lane. Hardback.

This is a highly satisfying book by one of my favourite historians,
Andrew Roberts, who has recently written enormous biographies of
Napoleon and Churchill. This book is of much more manageable length,
and features chapters on ten leaders in war. These leaders are

Napoleon, Horatio Nelson, Churchill, Hitler, Stalin, George Marshall,
Charles de Gaulle, Eisenhower and Margaret Thatcher. I enjoyed the
chapters on the lesser-known American figures the most (they added the
most to my knowledge) and very much enjoyed the judgement of Hitler as
an untalented ordinary man of no military genius whatsoever.

One of Roberts's first books is Masters and Commanders and covers
English WW2 figures like Alan Brooke and Montgomery; Brooke is the
fourth major figure in M&C along with Churchill, Roosevelt and
Marshall.

I read half of an excellent WW2 book by Roberts, The Storm of War, a
few years ago but kept putting it down and losing it in book
reorganisations; next time I see it I'll have to read it fully and
properly. I own most of his other books; each of them is more
substantial than Leadership in War but each should be a good read.

A very nice aspect of this book is that Roberts namedrops other
historians and recent work in the field; such as Stephen Kotkin.

Most of the time with Allen Lane hardback books I shelve them and
never read them and end up re-buying the same book when published in
orange creamy paperback a year later (depending on availability in
Lifelie sales). The only other Allen Lane hardback I've finised is an
even smaller book, Twilight of Democracy. There's nothing wrong with
reading books in hardback and it adds gravitas to the book being read.

